    "description": "Hospital management systems connect administrative and clinical information within a hospital. Iranian research highlights the importance of shared data definitions, usable interfaces and reliable integration across departments. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Hospital information systems", "Hospital management", "Usability", "Clinical workflows", "Interoperability"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:09:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:09:21Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Hospital Management Systems in Iran: Workflows and Usability",
      "description": "Explore Iranian hospital information systems, from shared clinical data to emergency workflows, implementation challenges and software transitions."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "hospital-information",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The information behind hospital operations",
        "content": "Hospital management systems are often discussed in Iranian research as hospital information systems, or HIS. Their information extends across administrative and clinical work. A national minimum-data-set study examined hospital records and software documentation to identify common information requirements, distinguishing administrative data from clinical documentation. The study demonstrates why a hospital needs shared definitions as well as computer terminals: information must remain understandable when it moves between departments or is used for reporting. Its proposed specification should not be read as proof of universal implementation. [s1]\n\nThe practical question is whether software supports the work being performed. An emergency department, for example, needs information that staff can enter and retrieve while responding to unpredictable admissions. A system’s presence alone says little about how well that workflow functions. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "emergency-workflows",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What frontline evaluations reveal",
        "content": "Research published in 2023 examined emergency information systems in public teaching hospitals in Kerman, drawing on nurses’ and information-technology staff members’ experiences. Reported problems included repeated manual documentation, difficulty finding patients, system crashes and absent decision support. Some accounts also described gaps between hospital software and departmental equipment or radiology processes. These are local findings, rather than a national failure rate. [s2]\n\nTheir wider significance is methodological: evaluation should follow an actual task through the system. A record that can technically be saved may still require duplicate entry or leave staff searching for information. The Kerman study therefore helps explain why users’ accounts are important alongside assessments of software features. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "implementation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Implementation involves people and institutions",
        "content": "An Iranian study published in 2024 identified social and technical obstacles to electronic-health implementation, including training costs, security concerns, platform diversity and difficulty integrating software into daily work. It also assessed hospital cases using the Electronic Medical Record Adoption Model, a framework for examining digital maturity. Findings from those cases cannot establish the maturity of every Iranian hospital. They nevertheless show why purchasing software is only part of implementation: staff preparation and organizational change also affect whether it becomes useful. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "software-transitions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "New platforms still need scrutiny",
        "content": "A study published in 2026 examined the transition from the desktop-based IHIS platform to the web-based Sepid platform at Qaem Hospital in Mashhad. It focused on admission and radiology, combining expert inspection with user assessment. Sepid received more favourable usability evaluations, but both platforms retained important weaknesses. Reported concerns included responsiveness, error feedback, navigation and integration with external databases. [s4]\n\nThis comparison offers a useful conclusion for understanding hospital modernization: a newer interface can improve experience while leaving substantial workflow problems unresolved. Because the study concerned a single teaching hospital and selected modules, it supports careful local evaluation rather than a nationwide ranking of products. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
