    "description": "Telemedicine in Iran includes patient-facing online consultation and remote consultation between clinicians. Research documents practical services alongside persistent questions about integration, professional preparation, financing and governance. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Telemedicine", "Remote consultation", "Digital health", "Clinical communication", "Health access"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:09:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:09:21Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Telemedicine in Iran: Online Care and Specialist Consultation",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s remote consultation models, evidence from the Moein programme, and research on access, integration and implementation barriers."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-models",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different ways to deliver care at a distance",
        "content": "Telemedicine uses communication technologies to deliver clinical services across distance. In Iran, it encompasses different relationships: a patient may consult a clinician through an online platform, while a doctor may seek specialist input about a patient already receiving local care. These models have different information needs and should not be treated as one uniform service. Iranian research examines both patient-facing online consultation and structured consultation between physicians. [s1][s2]\n\nThe distinction also helps explain apparently conflicting descriptions of implementation. A review published in 2025 characterized telemedicine as insufficiently embedded in formal services, but its evidence search covered studies only through March 2022. Later research describes an operational specialist-consultation programme. The older review is therefore evidence about implementation barriers, rather than a definitive inventory of services at the editorial cutoff. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "specialist-consultation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Specialist advice through the Moein programme",
        "content": "A 2026 study examined Iran’s Moein programme, which supported obstetrics and gynaecology consultation between physicians. In the setting studied during 2023–2024, referring and consulting clinicians discussed cases through live voice calls. Interviews highlighted information gathering, interpretation of the situation, shared decisions, ethical and emotional factors, and available resources. [s2]\n\nThe study makes clear that remote expertise depends on communication as well as connectivity. The consultant needs a useful account of the patient’s condition, while the referring clinician must interpret advice within the local clinical setting. This qualitative investigation explains participants’ experience; it does not establish a reduction in complications or prove that the model performs equally well across specialties. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "online-consultations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Online consultation and continuity of care",
        "content": "Research published in 2025 explored policymakers’ and administrators’ views of online medical consultation using interviews conducted in 2024. Participants saw potential advantages in access, follow-up and reduced travel burdens. They also described practical tensions: text conversations could take substantial time, clinicians needed preparation for remote practice, and platform services were not consistently integrated with established care pathways. These are stakeholder assessments, rather than measured national savings or clinical effects. [s1]\n\nFor readers assessing digital care, integration is a central question. The existence of an online encounter does not itself demonstrate that its information reaches the patient’s other providers. The study accordingly identifies connections with national information systems and clearer implementation arrangements as priorities. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "implementation-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the evidence can establish",
        "content": "The broader review grouped Iranian implementation barriers around governance, financing, the workforce and health information systems. A separate interview study with health policymakers similarly identified policy weaknesses, unclear operating mechanisms and difficulties changing established service models. Together, these sources explain why telemedicine cannot be understood simply as installing a communications application. [s3][s4]\n\nThe evidence supports a measured conclusion: remote consultation has documented uses in Iran, while sustainable expansion depends on organizational arrangements and professional practice. Claims about nationwide coverage, universal insurance reimbursement or improved population outcomes require additional evidence beyond these implementation studies. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
